WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.949
What the heck is the DOW?

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:06.370
For many people, “the DOW” is one of those
terms that when you hear it on TV or read

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:10.250
it in an article, your eyes glaze over and
your mind wanders to something more relevant

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.700
to your life, like whether you could fit 12
marshmallows in your mouth at once.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.270
But how do you know you don’t need to know
what the DOW is… when you don’t know what

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:18.640
the DOW is?

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:26.520
[MUSIC]

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.080
In the late 19th century, a newspaper editor
named Charles Dow and a statistician named

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.140
Edward Jones wanted to create a simple tool for how the U.S economy was doing day-to-day.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.340
Since most of the economy was industrial at
the time, they decided to combine the stock

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.800
prices of the twelve biggest industrial companies
into one statistic: the Dow Jones Industrial

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.800
Average.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.191
Those twelve companies were: American Cotton
Oil Company, American Sugar Company, American

00:00:51.191 --> 00:00:55.870
Tobacco Company, Chicago Gas Company, Distilling
&amp; Cattle Feeding Company, Laclede Gas Company,

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.980
National Lead Company, North American Company,
Tennessee Coal, Iron and Railroad Company,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.699
U.S. Leather Company, United States Rubber
Company, and the only one still on the DOW

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:06.260
today: General Electric.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.999
Today, 30 companies make up the DOW, and their
combined share prices are used as an index,

00:01:10.999 --> 00:01:15.140
or a benchmark, not just for how the stock
market is performing in general, but also

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.689
how specific companies are performing compared
to the market.

00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:22.939
So, for instance, if you own a blue-chip stock
that’s holding steady while the DOW is going

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.820
down, you can say that your stock is outperforming the market.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.080
But wait a minute. There's almost 4,000 companies being traded on the U.S. stock market.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.980
How could just 30 provide a reliable snapshot, no matter how big they are?

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.660
That’s a valid question.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:41.509
Some people think the DOW’s importance has
been blown way out of proportion.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:46.780
After all, these 30 companies can be doing
well when most Americans are hurting, like

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.159
in the case of massive layoffs.

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.549
And, as you’ve probably already noticed,
they do a LOT of business overseas, so their

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:56.590
share prices don’t necessarily reflect the
strength of the American economy.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.439
So why do cable news hosts still endlessly
refer to it as if it’s the be-all end-all

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:03.429
of market measurement?

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:06.670
Well, the stock market’s a funny thing.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:11.620
Since its gains and losses are driven largely
by whether people are deciding to buy or sell,

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:16.230
what’s actually happening in the economy
is less important than what people think is

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.230
happening.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.870
An individual stockbroker is trying to guess
what all the other stockbrokers are going

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:21.870
to do.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.690
But all the other stockbrokers are thinking
the exact same thing.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:29.060
Each investor needs to watch the DOW simply
because he or she knows that every other investor

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.340
is watching the DOW--the starting point of
an endless guessing game that often leads

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.550
to overreactive bubbles and plunges.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:41.120
Adam Davidson of the New York Times called
the DOW an “anxiety amplification device”.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.959
So do you need to worry about the DOW?

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:48.110
Should you track it like a hawk, updating
your 401(k) with every news story?

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.430
Or should you just ignore it?

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.430
Neither.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:55.799
Knowing what the DOW is… and isn’t, will
help protect you from the hysteria that can

00:02:55.799 --> 00:02:57.650
dominate the news in times of worry.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.019
But you should know that it’s just one of
many indicators of how things are going in

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:05.620
the U.S. economy - like GDP, employment numbers,
and Treasury Interest Rates.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.689
Maybe we should take a cue from the man who
it’s named for - Charles Dow was observed

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:12.469
to rarely look at the index himself.

00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:17.160
And the executive director of Dow Jones Indexes,
John Prestbo, recommended that the average

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.239
investor should only look at the index around
once a quarter, or once a month at the very

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:23.239
most.

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:26.810
So the next time you hear someone on cable
news yelling about the DOW… now that you

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.220
know what it is, you can probably go back
to what you were doing.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.360
And that’s our two cents!

